<section class="section">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="section-heading">
            <span class="eyebrow blue">COMMON WATER HEATER PROBLEMS</span>
            <h2>What it may mean—and how SAFE addresses it.</h2>
            <p>These are starting points, not remote diagnoses. Related searches are grouped on stronger pages instead of one URL per question.</p>
            <p class="seo-cluster-links">
                <a href="/no-hot-water/">No hot water</a>
                <a href="/water-heater-leaking/">Leaking</a>
                <a href="/water-heater-emergency/">Emergency</a>
                <a href="/repair-or-replace-water-heater/">Repair or replace</a>
                <a href="/electric-water-heater-not-working/">Electric not working</a>
                <a href="/gas-water-heater-not-working/">Gas not working</a>
                <a href="/not-enough-hot-water/">Runs out of hot water</a>
                <a href="/water-heater-making-noise/">Noises</a>
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="problem-guide-grid">
            <article class="problem-guide-card">
                <div class="problem-card-head"><span>01</span></div>
                <h3>No hot water</h3>
                <dl>
                    <div><dt>What you notice</dt><dd>The tank produces only cold or lukewarm water.</dd></div>
                    <div><dt>Possible causes</dt><dd>Tripped breaker, failed element or thermostat, ignition or pilot problem, gas-control issue or interrupted energy supply.</dd></div>
                    <div class="safe-response"><dt>How SAFE addresses it</dt><dd>We identify the unit and fuel, confirm visible settings and energy supply, review fault conditions and determine whether qualified component diagnosis or replacement is appropriate.</dd></div>
                </dl>
                <p class="seo-cluster-links"><a href="/no-hot-water/">No-hot-water guide</a> · <a href="/electric-water-heater-not-working/">Electric</a> · <a href="/gas-water-heater-not-working/">Gas</a></p>
            </article>
            <article class="problem-guide-card urgent">
                <div class="problem-card-head"><span>02</span><b>SAFETY PRIORITY</b></div>
                <h3>Water heater is leaking</h3>
                <dl>
                    <div><dt>What you notice</dt><dd>Water appears below the unit, in the drain pan or around a fitting.</dd></div>
                    <div><dt>Possible causes</dt><dd>Loose connection, shutoff or drain valve, T&amp;P discharge, condensation, corrosion or tank-shell failure.</dd></div>
                    <div class="safe-response"><dt>How SAFE addresses it</dt><dd>We locate the source first. Accessible valves or connections may be repairable; leakage from the tank shell normally requires replacement.</dd></div>
                </dl>
                <p class="seo-cluster-links"><a href="/water-heater-leaking/">Leak diagnosis</a> · <a href="/water-heater-emergency/">Emergency steps</a></p>
            </article>
            <article class="problem-guide-card">
                <div class="problem-card-head"><span>03</span></div>
                <h3>Hot water runs out quickly</h3>
                <dl>
                    <div><dt>What you notice</dt><dd>Showers turn cold sooner than normal or recovery is slow.</dd></div>
                    <div><dt>Possible causes</dt><dd>Failed lower element, thermostat problem, sediment, broken dip tube, undersized tank or increased household demand.</dd></div>
                    <div class="safe-response"><dt>How SAFE addresses it</dt><dd>We compare tank size and household demand, review recovery behavior and inspect the appropriate components before recommending repair or a correctly sized replacement.</dd></div>
                </dl>
                <p class="seo-cluster-links"><a href="/not-enough-hot-water/">Why hot water runs out</a> · <a href="/what-size-water-heater-do-i-need/">Sizing</a></p>
            </article>
            <article class="problem-guide-card urgent">
                <div class="problem-card-head"><span>04</span><b>SAFETY PRIORITY</b></div>
                <h3>Water is too hot</h3>
                <dl>
                    <div><dt>What you notice</dt><dd>Water temperature is unexpectedly high or presents a scald risk.</dd></div>
                    <div><dt>Possible causes</dt><dd>Incorrect setting, failed thermostat or control, wiring problem or unsafe control condition.</dd></div>
                    <div class="safe-response"><dt>How SAFE addresses it</dt><dd>We treat overheating as a safety issue, verify the control condition and avoid repeated resets. Qualifying controls require technician diagnosis.</dd></div>
                </dl>
                <p class="seo-cluster-links"><a href="/not-enough-hot-water/">Temperature problems</a> · <a href="/water-heater-emergency/">If it is steaming</a></p>
            </article>
            <article class="problem-guide-card">
                <div class="problem-card-head"><span>05</span></div>
                <h3>Popping, rumbling or banging</h3>
                <dl>
                    <div><dt>What you notice</dt><dd>Noise occurs while the burner or elements heat the tank.</dd></div>
                    <div><dt>Possible causes</dt><dd>Sediment buildup, mineral scale, overheating or expansion-related movement.</dd></div>
                    <div class="safe-response"><dt>How SAFE addresses it</dt><dd>We document when the noise occurs, review tank age and condition, and determine whether a sediment assessment, service or replacement is the safer value.</dd></div>
                </dl>
                <p class="seo-cluster-links"><a href="/water-heater-making-noise/">Noise guide</a></p>
            </article>
            <article class="problem-guide-card">
                <div class="problem-card-head"><span>06</span></div>
                <h3>Rusty, cloudy or smelly hot water</h3>
                <dl>
                    <div><dt>What you notice</dt><dd>Discoloration or odor appears mainly at hot-water fixtures.</dd></div>
                    <div><dt>Possible causes</dt><dd>Tank corrosion, anode condition, bacteria, water chemistry, mineral content or trapped air.</dd></div>
                    <div class="safe-response"><dt>How SAFE addresses it</dt><dd>We compare the hot and cold sides, inspect visible corrosion and evaluate the unit’s condition before choosing maintenance, component work or replacement.</dd></div>
                </dl>
                <p class="seo-cluster-links"><a href="/smelly-discolored-hot-water/">Smell and color guide</a></p>
            </article>
            <article class="problem-guide-card urgent">
                <div class="problem-card-head"><span>07</span><b>SAFETY PRIORITY</b></div>
                <h3>Breaker keeps tripping</h3>
                <dl>
                    <div><dt>What you notice</dt><dd>The electric water heater repeatedly loses power.</dd></div>
                    <div><dt>Possible causes</dt><dd>Failed element, thermostat, damaged wiring, moisture or another electrical fault.</dd></div>
                    <div class="safe-response"><dt>How SAFE addresses it</dt><dd>We advise against repeated resets. The unit should remain off until qualified electrical and water-heater diagnosis identifies the fault.</dd></div>
                </dl>
                <p class="seo-cluster-links"><a href="/electric-water-heater-not-working/">Electric not working</a></p>
            </article>
            <article class="problem-guide-card urgent">
                <div class="problem-card-head"><span>08</span><b>SAFETY PRIORITY</b></div>
                <h3>Pilot will not stay lit</h3>
                <dl>
                    <div><dt>What you notice</dt><dd>A gas tank does not ignite or the pilot repeatedly goes out.</dd></div>
                    <div><dt>Possible causes</dt><dd>Ignition or thermocouple problem, gas-control fault, combustion-air issue or venting condition.</dd></div>
                    <div class="safe-response"><dt>How SAFE addresses it</dt><dd>We document the fault and route gas-control, burner and venting diagnosis to a qualified technician. Gas odor requires immediate evacuation and utility contact.</dd></div>
                </dl>
                <p class="seo-cluster-links"><a href="/gas-water-heater-not-working/">Gas not working</a> · <a href="/water-heater-emergency/">Gas emergency</a></p>
            </article>
            <article class="problem-guide-card urgent">
                <div class="problem-card-head"><span>09</span><b>SAFETY PRIORITY</b></div>
                <h3>Relief valve continuously discharges</h3>
                <dl>
                    <div><dt>What you notice</dt><dd>Hot water repeatedly drains from the T&amp;P discharge pipe.</dd></div>
                    <div><dt>Possible causes</dt><dd>Excessive temperature, high water pressure, thermal expansion or valve failure.</dd></div>
                    <div class="safe-response"><dt>How SAFE addresses it</dt><dd>We never cap the pipe. SAFE checks the system conditions and determines whether pressure, expansion or valve correction is required.</dd></div>
                </dl>
                <p class="seo-cluster-links"><a href="/water-heater-emergency/">T&amp;P emergency</a> · <a href="/water-heater-code-upgrades/">Code extras</a></p>
            </article>
            <article class="problem-guide-card">
                <div class="problem-card-head"><span>10</span></div>
                <h3>Water pressure or expansion problems</h3>
                <dl>
                    <div><dt>What you notice</dt><dd>Relief-valve dripping, banging, fluctuating pressure or repeated connection leaks.</dd></div>
                    <div><dt>Possible causes</dt><dd>High supply pressure, a closed plumbing system, failed or missing expansion tank or improper tank charge.</dd></div>
                    <div class="safe-response"><dt>How SAFE addresses it</dt><dd>A qualified technician measures pressure, reviews the plumbing system and sizes or charges the expansion tank correctly when needed.</dd></div>
                </dl>
                <p class="seo-cluster-links"><a href="/low-hot-water-pressure/">Low pressure</a> · <a href="/water-heater-code-upgrades/">Expansion tank / PRV</a></p>
            </article>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container repair-decision">
        <div>
            <span class="eyebrow blue">REPAIR OR REPLACE?</span>
            <h2>Age alone does not decide.</h2>
            <p>SAFE combines tank-shell leakage, corrosion, repeat failures, parts availability, safety, operating cost and capacity fit. A leaking tank shell generally requires replacement—not repair. <a href="/repair-or-replace-water-heater/">Read the full decision guide</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="decision-cards">
            <article>
                <strong>Repair may fit</strong>
                <span>Newer tank • replaceable component • sound tank shell • reasonable cost</span>
            </article>
            <article>
                <strong>Replacement may fit</strong>
                <span>Tank-shell leak • advanced corrosion • repeated failures • unsafe or undersized unit</span>
            </article>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

